WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.775
NULL is one of the SQL ideas that often surprises
beginners NULL

00:00:04.788 --> 00:00:08.712
represents a missing or unknown value It is not the
same as

00:00:08.738 --> 00:00:13.162
zero an empty string or the word null stored as text
Because

00:00:13.188 --> 00:00:18.962
NULL represents unknown information ordinary
equality does not test it correctly Writing

00:00:18.988 --> 00:00:24.550
discharge_date equals NULL is not the right pattern
Instead SQL provides

00:00:24.575 --> 00:00:29.025
the special predicates IS NULL and IS NOT NULL In
our admissions

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:32.525
table a missing discharge date can indicate that an
admission is still

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:38.062
open The query selects admission information where
discharge_date IS NULL

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:41.888
That gives us the rows that do not currently have a
discharge

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:47.138
date Run the example and inspect the result Notice
that we are

00:00:47.162 --> 00:00:51.262
asking a clear data question which admissions have
no discharge date recorded

00:00:52.138 --> 00:00:57.025
This pattern is very common in data preparation
Missing values can affect

00:00:57.038 --> 00:01:02.612
filtering calculations and analysis so you need to
identify them correctly A

00:01:02.638 --> 00:01:06.050
simple rule to remember is when checking whether a
SQL value is

00:01:06.062 --> 00:01:10.550
missing use IS NULL When checking that a value is
present use

00:01:10.575 --> 00:01:14.788
IS NOT NULL Do not use equals NULL or not-equal NULL
as

00:01:14.812 --> 00:01:16.462
if NULL were an ordinary value
